      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}(2\\sqrt{x}-3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2\\sqrt{x}-3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}2\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4}-{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2})-({4}^{3}-{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(8-1)-(64-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{3}-63 \\\\  & =-\\frac{161}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
